const n=10,e="Fusarium Wilt of Guava (Psidium guajava L.): Etiology, Epidemiology, Molecular Biology, and Management Strategies",i="Shubham Patel, Hemant Kumar Singh, Ramesh Singh, Vivek Singh, Jitendra Kumar",a=[120,130],o=["1 Department of Plant Pathology, College of Agriculture, Acharya Narendra Deva University of Agriculture and Technology, Kumarganj, Ayodhya (U.P.) 224229.","2 Department of Plant Pathology, T.D.P.G. College Jaunpur (U.P.) 222002","Corresponding e-mail: shubham.pathologist@gmail.com"],t=[{t:"h",x:"Abstract:"},{t:"p",x:"Guava (Psidium guajava L.) ranks as a commercially significant tropical fruit crop, holding the fourth position globally in fruit production. In India, this crop faces a serious threat from a destructive vascular wilt disease caused by Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii (Fop). The pathogen persists in soil through chlamydospores for extended periods, leading to symptoms such as foliar yellowing, wilting, and vascular discoloration. Affected orchards typically experience disease incidence ranging from 15% to over 41%. The disease severity increases further through synergistic interactions with the root-knot nematode Meloidogyne enterolobii, which damages roots, modifies root exudate profiles, and compromises host defenses. This chapter provides a comprehensive overview of guava Fusarium wilt, covering pathogen taxonomy, morphology, molecular diversity, disease symptomatology, and epidemiological aspects. We also delve into the molecular mechanisms of pathogenesis, including cell-wall-degrading enzymes, mycotoxins, and effector proteins that suppress host immunity. The current status of resistance screening within the genus Psidium and host defense responses are also discussed. The chapter concludes with an integrated pest management (IPM) framework combining cultural practices, biological control agents like Trichoderma spp., Aspergillus niger, Pseudomonas fluorescens, and Bacillus subtilis, along with chemical measures and resistant rootstocks. Despite progress in molecular diagnostics and biological control, several gaps remain, including the need for whole-genome sequencing of Fop, identification of resistance genes, and development of stable bioformulations. Emerging approaches such as CRISPR-based gene editing and host-induced gene silencing offer promising future directions. This chapter aims to serve as a valuable resource for researchers and plant pathologists working on this devastating disease. Keywords: guava wilt, Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii, Meloidogyne enterolobii, disease complex, integrated disease management, biological control, vascular wilt, host resistance, molecular pathogenesis 1. Introduction:"},{t:"p",x:'Guava (Psidium guajava L.) is a tropical and subtropical fruit crop originating from the Americas. Often referred to as the "apple of the tropics" due to its nutritional value, guava ranks fourth in global fruit production, following mango, banana, and citrus. The species has a somatic chromosome number of 2n = 22, predominantly tetraploid. Major producing nations include India, China, Thailand, Pakistan, Brazil, Mexico, Indonesia, Bangladesh, the Philippines, and Nigeria. Within India, guava is the second-most important commercial fruit crop, cultivated extensively across tropical and subtropical regions. Although guava is considered a hardy crop capable of growing on marginal soils with minimal inputs, it remains highly susceptible to a wilt disease complex that poses a serious phytosanitary threat to production (Mishra et al., 2013). The primary causal agent is Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii (Cke. & Massee) Nair, Mehta & Subramanian (Fop), which induces a progressive vascular wilt characterized by yellowing, wilting, and vascular browning. Disease incidence ranges from 15% to over 41% in various guava-growing regions'},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 11"},{t:"p",x:"Management of Sclerotinia stem rot of Indian mustard (Brassica juncia)"},{t:"p",x:"(Gangaraj et al., 2024). Management becomes exceedingly difficult once infection occurs, as the pathogen survives in soil for prolonged periods as chlamydospores."},{t:"c",x:"Fig. 1: Brown streaks and necrosis in vascular tissues"},{t:"c",x:"Fig. 2: Stunt, wilting, and poor canopy development"},{t:"c",x:"Fig. 3: Leaf yellowing, defoliation and die-back of branches"},{t:"p",x:"A critical aspect of this disease is its synergistic interaction with the root-knot nematode Meloidogyne enterolobii. This nematode exacerbates disease severity by damaging root tissues, altering root exudate chemistry, and suppressing plant defense responses, thereby increasing host susceptibility to fungal infection (Shravani et al., 2025; Souza et al., 2023)."},{t:"p",x:"This chapter presents a systematic overview of guava wilt, covering pathogen characterization, disease epidemiology, molecular pathogenesis, and management strategies. Our objective is to provide a comprehensive resource for researchers investigating this destructive disease."},{t:"h",x:"2. Historical background and worldwide distribution:"},{t:"p",x:"Guava wilt was first documented in Taiwan by Kurosawa (1926), who attributed it to the fungus Myxosporium psidii. The disease was subsequently recorded in India in 1935 and has since been reported from nearly all guava-producing countries, including South Africa, Brazil, Egypt, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Thailand, Mexico, and parts of sub-Saharan Africa (Mishra et al., 2013)."},{t:"p",x:"The taxonomy of the causal agent has evolved considerably over time. Various fungi have been implicated, including Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii, F. solani, Gliocladium roseum, Cephalosporium sp., and Nalanthamala psidii (syn. Myxosporium psidii). Recent molecular and pathological studies have confirmed Fop as the primary agent of Fusarium wilt, while F. solani and F. falciforme have been associated with chlamydosporum wilt in certain regions (Gangaraj et al., 2024)."},{t:"p",x:"In India, guava wilt has been reported from major guava-growing states, including Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Bihar, Karnataka, and West Bengal. Surveys conducted during 2023–2024 revealed average disease incidence of 29.26%, with some blocks in Prayagraj and Kaushambi districts recording up to 41.43% (Gangaraj et al., 2024)."},{t:"l",x:["3. Taxonomy, Morphology, and Biology of the Pathogen:","3.1 Taxonomic Identification","Kingdom: Fungi","Phylum: Ascomycetes","Class: Hypocreales","Order: Nectriaceae"]},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 11"},{t:"p",x:"Management of Sclerotinia stem rot of Indian mustard (Brassica juncia)"},{t:"p",x:"Genus: Fusarium spp."},{t:"h",x:"Species: Fusarium oxysporum"},{t:"p",x:'Fusarium oxysporum represents an anamorphic species complex. The term "forma specialis" (f. sp.) denotes physiological specialization based on pathogenicity to specific host plants rather than formal taxonomic rank. Fop is distinguished by its unique virulence toward P. guajava. This host-specific virulence is governed by avirulence and virulence genes located on lineage-specific (LS) chromosomes, which can be transferred horizontally between F. oxysporum genotypes (Epstein et al., 2022; Ma et al., 2010).'},{t:"h",x:"3.2 Morphological Characteristics:"},{t:"p",x:"On potato dextrose agar (PDA), Fop colonies appear white to pale pink or lavender, with abundant aerial mycelium and a purple to violet reverse. The fungus grows optimally at 25–30°C and pH 6.0–7.0, although it tolerates a broader pH range of 5.0–8.5. Maximum mycelial dry weight (549.67 mg) and sporulation occur at pH 6.5 (Shravani et al., 2025)."},{t:"p",x:"Like other F. oxysporum formal speciales, Fop produces three conidial types:"},{t:"p",x:"Microconidia: These are single-celled (occasionally one-septate), oval to ellipsoid, borne in false heads from phialides. Microconidia are the most abundant type and play a primary role in xylem colonization. Macroconidia: These are spindle-shaped, hyaline, 3–5 septate, with a pointed apical cell and a foot-shaped basal cell. They develop on host surfaces and in culture within sporodochia, measuring 25–50 µm in length and 3–5 µm in width."},{t:"p",x:"Chlamydospores: These are terminal or intercalary thick-walled spores, occurring singly or in pairs. Them melanized walls confer resistance to desiccation and fungicides, enabling long-term survival in soil and facilitating disease transmission (Dean et al., 2012; Ploetz, 2015)."},{t:"h",x:"3.3 Molecular Diversity and Characterization:"},{t:"p",x:"Molecular tools have significantly enhanced Fop identification and characterization. Mishra et al. (2013) developed species-specific primers based on conserved regions of the 28S rDNA and intergenic spacer (IGS) sequences, enabling discrimination of Fop from other Fusarium species infecting guava. RAPD marker analysis of 21 Fop isolates from diverse Indian agro-climatic regions revealed high polymorphism (22–74%), grouping isolates into three major clusters and suggesting geographic structuring of genetic diversity. PCR-RFLP analysis of the ITS1-5.8S-ITS2 region provides a valuable tool for species-level identification and population genetic studies. This approach facilitates early, pre-symptomatic diagnosis of Fusarium wilt and helps elucidate the distribution and host relationships of different Fusarium populations."},{t:"h",x:"4. Symptoms of the Disease:"},{t:"h",x:"4.1 Foliar Symptoms:"},{t:"p",x:"Fusarium wilt of guava is a progressive, typically fatal disease with a distinct symptom progression. Initial symptoms generally appear during the early rainy season (August–October in the Indian subcontinent), favoured by high soil moisture and humidity. Disease progression occurs in three stages: Stage 1 – Early Wilting: Mild chlorosis (yellowing) appears on leaves of one or more shoots, beginning at the tips and margins. Affected leaves wilt during midday but may recover in the evening, similar to early Fusarium wilt symptoms in other crops."},{t:"p",x:"Stage 2 – Progressive Dieback: Chlorotic leaves become necrotic, turning uniformly brown while remaining attached to branches—a characteristic vascular wilt symptom. Branch and twig dieback progresses acropetally (from base to tip), accompanied by premature leaf fall."},{t:"p",x:"Stage 3 – Total Wilt: The entire canopy wilts, with water-soaked bark and sunken lesions developing. Infected plants fail to produce fruit and typically die within weeks to months, depending on isolate virulence, environmental conditions, and presence of associated pathogens."},{t:"h",x:"4.2 Internal Symptoms:"},{t:"p",x:"Vascular browning serves as a diagnostic internal symptom distinguishing Fusarium wilt from other wilting diseases. Cross-sections of roots, stem bases, or main branches reveal distinct brown to dark brown discoloration of xylem tissue. This discoloration results from fungal colonization, tylosis formation, and deposition of phenolic compounds as host defense responses. In severe cases, vascular browning extends from roots to the main stem and branches."},{t:"h",x:"4.3 Root Symptoms and the Fusarium-Nematode Complex:"},{t:"p",x:"Infection with root-knot nematodes (Meloidogyne enterolobii and M. incognita) produces characteristic root galls ranging from small bead-like swellings to large, confluent masses. Root rot and necrosis caused by Fusarium may be masked by nematode-induced galling. Co-infected plants exhibit more severe root rot than those infected with either pathogen alone (Shravani et al., 2025; Souza et al., 2023). This enhanced severity occurs because root-knot nematodes compromise root epidermal and cortical integrity, facilitating fungal entry, while also altering root exudates to promote Fusarium proliferation."},{t:"h",x:"5. Epidemiology and Disease Cycle:"},{t:"h",x:"5.1 Inoculum and Soil Persistence:"},{t:"p",x:"Chlamydospores constitute the primary inoculum of Fop, enabling prolonged survival in soil, particularly in the presence of host plants. These resistant spores tolerate desiccation, wide pH ranges, and fungicides, and germinate to produce hyphae that penetrate root tips or wounds, colonizing the root cortex and xylem vessels. Although macroconidia and microconidia produced on plant debris and in the rhizosphere can also initiate infection, they are less persistent and play a secondary role."},{t:"h",x:"5.2 Infection and Colonization Sequence:"},{t:"p",x:"Following germination and penetration, Fop colonizes the root cortex and advances through the endodermis into xylem vessels. Within the xylem, the fungus produces microconidia that are transported upward, colonizing other plant parts. Macroconidia and mycelium further contribute to xylem colonization. In response, the host produces tyloses, gels, gums, and polyphenolic compounds that restrict but do not prevent fungal spread. Pathogenesis-related (PR) proteins also limit colonization. However, in susceptible varieties, these defenses prove inadequate, allowing systemic colonization that culminates in wilting, chlorosis, dieback, and eventual plant death over several weeks to months."},{t:"h",x:"5.3 Dispersal Mechanisms:"},{t:"p",x:"Secondary spread of Fop within and between orchards occurs primarily through movement of infested soil (via implements, irrigation water, infected planting material, or root contact between neighbouring trees). Wetter, compacted soils favor root fusion, facilitating transmission. Wind dispersal of macroconidia may also contribute to local spread. At regional scales, human activities such as planting or soil preparation remain the primary means of long-distance dissemination."},{t:"h",x:"5.4 Environmental Factors Influencing Disease Development:"},{t:"h",x:"Several environmental factors affect disease severity:"},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 11"},{t:"p",x:"Management of Sclerotinia stem rot of Indian mustard (Brassica juncia)"},{t:"p",x:"Temperature: Optimal disease development occurs at 25–30°C, a temperature range typical of tropical and subtropical guava-growing regions, especially during monsoon and post-monsoon periods. Soil pH: Alkaline soils (pH 7.5–9.0) favor disease, although the fungus can grow across a wide pH range (5.0–8.5), with optimal growth at pH 6.5."},{t:"p",x:"Soil Type: Poorly drained clay loam and sandy loam soils increase disease incidence by maintaining high moisture levels conducive to chlamydospore germination, root exudation, and root-to-root contact. Rainfall: Symptom expression increases sharply with the onset of the monsoon, likely due to elevated soil moisture."},{t:"p",x:"Orchard Age: All age groups are susceptible, but orchards aged 2–5 years exhibit higher mortality rates, possibly due to concurrent M. enterolobii infection."},{t:"h",x:"6. The Fusarium-Nematode Disease Complex:"},{t:"h",x:"6.1 Biology of Meloidogyne enterolobii:"},{t:"p",x:"Meloidogyne enterolobii (syn. M. mayaguensis) is a highly destructive root-knot nematode capable of overcoming the Mi 1.2 resistance gene effective against other Meloidogyne species. First reported on guava in India in 2016, it has since been documented in other guava-growing states and countries, including Egypt (first reported in March 2023). This nematode causes severe damage to P. guajava due to its high reproductive potential and aggressiveness."},{t:"h",x:"6.2 Mechanisms of Synergistic Interaction:"},{t:"p",x:"Greenhouse and molecular studies have confirmed the synergistic association between M. enterolobii and Fop. Sequential inoculation experiments by Shravani et al. (2025) demonstrated that pre-inoculation with nematodes (14 days before fungal inoculation) resulted in the highest disease severity and greatest reduction in plant growth, compared to simultaneous or reverse inoculation, indicating that nematode feeding predisposes plants to wilt infection."},{t:"h",x:"This synergistic interaction involves multiple mechanisms:"},{t:"p",x:"Mechanical damage to root epidermis and cortex by second-stage juveniles (J2), facilitating fungal penetration;"},{t:"p",x:"Disruption of vascular tissues through giant cell and gall formation;"},{t:"p",x:"Alteration of root exudate composition, providing increased carbon and nitrogen that stimulate fungal sporulation (Souza et al., 2023; van Gundy et al., 1977);"},{t:"p",x:'Suppression of host defense responses in galled tissues, reducing resistance to subsequent Fop infection. Souza et al. (2023) demonstrated that root exudates from nematode-infested roots, but not from healthy roots, triggered fungal infection in a host-specific manner, effectively "unlocking" resistant hosts. Metabolomic analysis identified amino acids, sugars, and phenolics as key active compounds. While this study involved Neocosmospora falciformis, similar mechanisms likely mediate the M. enterolobii–Fop interaction.'},{t:"h",x:"7. Molecular Pathogenesis:"},{t:"h",x:"7.1 Genome Architecture of F. oxysporum:"},{t:"p",x:'The reference F. oxysporum genome comprises a ~40 Mb conserved "core" genome encoding essential functions, along with four lineage-specific (LS) chromosomes that harbor most pathogenicity genes. The LS compartment is enriched in transposable elements, repeats, and genes under positive selection, reflecting host-driven evolutionary pressures. LS chromosomes can be acquired through horizontal chromosome transfer (HCT) between strains, facilitating rapid virulence evolution. Acquisition of LS chromosomes 3, 6, 14, and other regions by non-pathogenic endophytic strains was critical for pathogenicity emergence (Ma et al., 2010; Epstein et al., 2022). Although the Fop genome has not yet been sequenced, comparative analyses suggest a similar architecture.'},{t:"h",x:"7.2 Virulence Determinants:"},{t:"p",x:"Fop pathogenicity depends on various virulence factors, including cell-wall-degrading enzymes (CWDEs), mycotoxins, and secreted in xylem (SIX) proteins."},{t:"p",x:"Cell-Wall-Degrading Enzymes (CWDEs): Polygalacturonases, pectate lyases, xylanases, and cutinases sequentially degrade pectin, hemicellulose, and cutin during invasive growth, causing cell separation and xylem maceration. Expression of genes encoding these enzymes is regulated by transcription factors Xln1, Ctf1, and Ctf2 (Zuriegat et al., 2021)."},{t:"p",x:"Mycotoxins: Fusaric acid and beauvericin are major mycotoxins produced by pathogenic Fo isolates (López Díaz et al., 2018; Gangaraj et al., 2024). Fusaric acid induces plant cell death by disrupting membranes and inhibiting metal-dependent defense enzymes. Beauvericin is a cyclic depsipeptide ionophore that disrupts mitochondrial membrane potential and triggers programmed cell death. Gangaraj et al. (2024) detected fusaric acid in vascular tissues of infected guava plants."},{t:"p",x:"SIX Effector Proteins: Secreted in xylem (SIX) proteins are small cysteine-rich effectors that play central roles in pathogenesis and host specificity. They suppress pattern-triggered immunity (PTI) and effector-triggered immunity (ETI), inhibit reactive oxygen species (ROS) production, disrupt salicylic acid (SA) and jasmonic acid (JA) signaling, and suppress pathogenesis-related (PR) gene expression (van Dam et al., 2015; Zuriegat et al., 2021). In F. oxysporum f. sp. lycopersici (Fol), 14 SIX proteins (SIX1–SIX14) have been characterized, with SIX4 (AVR1), SIX3 (AVR2), and SIX1 (AVR3) recognized by host resistance genes (van Dam et al., 2015). The overall host range of Fo is determined by the combination of effectors and their ability to overcome host defenses."},{t:"p",x:"Proteomic analysis of xylem exudates from Fop-infected guava has identified ~14 pathogenicity-related compounds, but specific protein families utilized by Fop remain under investigation."},{t:"h",x:"7.3 Virulence-Related Transcription Factors:"},{t:"p",x:"The Fo genome encodes over 700 transcription factors (TFs), with 26 experimentally characterized from different formal speciales. Of these, 23 TFs from 10 families play roles in pathogenicity. Sge1 (Six Gene Expression 1), an ortholog of Wor1, is the master regulator of SIX genes; its deletion severely reduces virulence and xylem colonization (Zuriegat et al., 2021). Zinc finger TFs constitute the largest pathogenicity-related group. Other TFs involved in secondary metabolite biosynthesis include FolCzf1 and the velvet complex (VelB, VeA, LaeA). TFs regulating CWDEs include Xln1, Ctf1, and Ctf2 (Zuriegat et al., 2021)."},{t:"h",x:"8. Host-Pathogen Interaction and Host Resistance:"},{t:"h",x:"8.1 Host Defense Responses:"},{t:"p",x:"Guava deploys multifaceted defenses against Fop invasion, including anatomical, biochemical, and molecular mechanisms:"},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 11"},{t:"p",x:"Management of Sclerotinia stem rot of Indian mustard (Brassica juncia)"},{t:"p",x:"Anatomical defenses: Xylem vessels undergo tylosis and gum deposition, while callose, lignin, and suberin accumulate to block pathogen spread."},{t:"p",x:"Biochemical defenses: The phenylpropanoid pathway produces phytoalexins and phenolic acids that directly combat fungal cells. Defense-related enzymes include chitinase, glucanases, peroxidase, PAL, and SOD. Molecular defenses: SA, JA, and ethylene signalling cascades activate defense responses, upregulating PR1, PR3, PR4, PR5, and PR10 genes. However, these responses often prove insufficient due to Fop effectors that suppress PTI and ETI components."},{t:"h",x:"8.2 Resistance Screening:"},{t:"p",x:"Screening of Psidium germplasm for resistance is an active research area. Vishwakarma et al. (2023) identified Psidium species and interspecific hybrids resistant to both Fop and southern root-knot nematode. Wild species such as P. cattleianum, P. friedrichsthalianum, and P. guineense are often resistant or immune to Fop compared to cultivated P. guajava, making them valuable sources of resistance for breeding programs."},{t:"h",x:"9. Integrated Pest Management (IPM):"},{t:"p",x:"Managing Fop is challenging due to its soilborne nature and long-term chlamydospore survival. An integrated approach combining cultural, biological, chemical, and host resistance strategies offer the most sustainable solution."},{t:"l",x:["9.1 Cultural and Physical Practices:","Key cultural practices include:","Using pathogen-free nursery stock;","Avoiding replanting in infested sites;"]},{t:"p",x:"Burning infected plant material (including roots) to eliminate inoculum;"},{t:"p",x:"Soil solarization (covering wet soil with plastic sheets during warm weather) to kill pathogens and nematodes; Crop rotation with allelopathic or non-host plants (e.g., marigold);"},{t:"p",x:"Maintaining optimal soil pH (5.5–6.5);"},{t:"p",x:"Improving drainage to reduce soil moisture;"},{t:"p",x:"Sanitizing tools and equipment to prevent mechanical transmission."},{t:"h",x:"9.2 Chemical Management:"},{t:"p",x:"Chemical control has yielded mixed results, as contact fungicides cannot reach vascular pathogens, and systemic fungicides undergo adsorption and metabolism before reaching target sites. In vitro tests show that propiconazole, tebuconazole, and carbendazim inhibit Fop mycelial growth by 100% at 50 ppm. However, field applications of carbendazim as soil drenches or stem injections have produced inconsistent results, and carbendazim resistance is a growing concern. Nematicides like carbofuran and fenamiphos reduce nematode populations and wilt severity, but their use is restricted due to environmental and health concerns."},{t:"h",x:"9.3 Biological Control:"},{t:"h",x:"Biological control has gained attention as a sustainable alternative:"},{t:"p",x:"Trichoderma spp.: Species such as T. harzianum, T. virens, and T. viride employ mycoparasitism (cell wall degradation via cellulases, chitinases, glucanases), antibiosis (production of trichodermin, harzianic acid, peptaibols), resource competition, and induced systemic resistance (ISR). Dual culture assays show 58–65% inhibition of Fop mycelial growth, with some isolates exceeding 70%. Field trials have demonstrated disease suppression when applied as soil amendments or seed treatments."},{t:"p",x:"Aspergillus niger: Gangaraj et al. (2024) showed that strain AN 11 inhibited Fop, F. falciforme, F. chlamydosporum, and F. verticillioides by up to 86.33% in vitro. UPLC-QTOF-ESI-MS profiling identified 41 secondary metabolites with antifungal activity, including kotanin, isokotanin A, kojic acid, pyranonigrin, and chloramphenicol, providing a foundation for bioformulation development."},{t:"p",x:"Pseudomonas fluorescens: These PGPR triggers ISR via the JA/ethylene pathway and produces 2,4-diacetyl phloroglucinol, pyoluteorin, and siderophores that directly suppress fungal pathogens. Bacillus subtilis: Produces lipopeptide antibiotics (iturin, fengycin, surfactin) that disrupt fungal membranes, inhibit spore germination, and prevent mycelial growth, along with extracellular enzymes (chitinases, glucanases, proteases). Combinations of Trichoderma, Pseudomonas, and Bacillus often perform better than single organisms due to synergistic interactions in the rhizosphere."},{t:"h",x:"9.4 Rootstocks and Host Resistance:"},{t:"p",x:"Resistant rootstocks offer the most durable management option. Field trials with interspecific hybrids of P. guajava and wild Psidium spp. have demonstrated tolerance to Fop. P. cattleianum and P. friedrichsthalianum are reported tolerant to both Fop and M. enterolobii, making them promising rootstock candidates. Grafting commercial varieties onto resistant rootstocks is an economical strategy practiced in several countries, including Brazil. Vishwakarma et al. (2023) identified interspecific hybrid lines with quantitative resistance to Fop and southern root-knot nematode."},{t:"h",x:"9.5 Integrated Disease Management Modules:"},{t:"p",x:"No single strategy adequately controls guava wilt. An evidence-based IDM framework includes: Use of disease-free planting material on resistant rootstocks;"},{t:"p",x:"Soil solarization with cruciferous green manure for heavily infested fields;"},{t:"p",x:"Application of Trichoderma bioformulations (2.5–5 kg/tree) at planting and at monsoon onset; Rhizosphere treatment with P. fluorescens or B. subtilis bioformulations;"},{t:"p",x:"Soil drenching with systemic fungicides (carbendazim, propiconazole) for high-risk orchards as remedial measures;"},{t:"p",x:"Nematode management using bionematicides (Paecilomyces lilacinus, Purpureocillium lilacinum), organic manures, or marigold intercropping;"},{t:"p",x:"Prompt removal and destruction of diseased plants."},{t:"h",x:"10. Research Needs and Knowledge Gaps:"},{t:"p",x:"Despite progress, several gaps remain for developing durable guava wilt management strategies: Whole-genome sequencing of Fop: A fully annotated Fop genome would enable identification of effector repertoires, LS chromosomes, and secondary metabolite gene clusters unique to this forma specialis."},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 11"},{t:"p",x:"Management of Sclerotinia stem rot of Indian mustard (Brassica juncia)"},{t:"p",x:"Molecular basis of host resistance: R-genes and QTLs conferring resistance in Psidium remain uncharacterized. QTL analysis and RNA-seq transcriptomics of resistant and susceptible genotypes would facilitate marker-assisted breeding."},{t:"p",x:"Molecular basis of Fusarium-nematode synergism: The metabolite signals in nematode-parasitized root exudates that induce Fop chlamydospore germination and virulence gene upregulation need characterization. Multi-omics (metabolome + transcriptome) profiling of the tripartite interaction would elucidate this signaling. Bioformulation development and delivery: Commercial Trichoderma and PGPR bioformulations often show inconsistent field efficacy due to poor establishment and pH sensitivity. Novel delivery technologies (nano-encapsulation, microbial consortia, slow-release polymers) should be explored. CRISPR and RNAi approaches: Host-induced gene silencing (HIGS) targeting conserved Fop virulence factors (SIX effectors, transcription factors Sge1, Xln1) offers cutting-edge opportunities. CRISPR/Cas9 genome engineering could generate durable resistance in susceptible guava accessions."},{t:"h",x:"11. Conclusions and Summary:"},{t:"p",x:"Fusarium wilt of guava, caused by F. oxysporum f. sp. psidii, represents one of the most economically damaging disease complexes in global guava production, particularly when combined with the root-knot nematode M. enterolobii. The disease features progressive vascular wilt, driven by an arsenal of virulence factors including CWDEs, mycotoxins, and SIX effectors that suppress plant immunity and facilitate vascular colonization."},{t:"p",x:"Recent molecular diagnostic advances have improved pathogen taxonomy and population genetics, with implications for resistance breeding and sustainability. Understanding of Fusarium-nematode synergism has advanced, revealing nematode-induced changes in root exudates that facilitate Fusarium parasitism. The discovery of A. niger metabolites with activity against multiple wilt pathogens (Gangaraj et al., 2024) opens new avenues for biocontrol formulation."},{t:"p",x:"Sustainable management of guava wilt will require integrated approaches combining resistant/tolerant rootstocks, microbiome-informed biocontrol agents, cultural practices, and judicious chemical use, with consideration of abiotic factors. The impending availability of the Fop genome sequence will undoubtedly accelerate discovery of novel therapeutic targets based on pathogen biology and evolutionary relationships."},{t:"h",x:"References:"},{t:"p",x:"Dean, R., Van Kan, J. A. L., Pretorius, Z. A., Hammond-Kosack, K. E., Di Pietro, A., Spanu, P. D., Rudd, J. J., Dickman, M., Kahmann, R., Ellis, J., & Foster, G. D. (2012). The Top 10 fungal pathogens in molecular plant pathology. Molecular Plant Pathology, 13(4), 414–430. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1364- 3703.2011.00783.x"},{t:"p",x:"Epstein, L., Kaur, S., & Henry, P. M. (2022). The emergence of Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. apii race 4 and Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. coriandrii highlights major obstacles facing agricultural production in coastal California in a warming climate: A case study. Frontiers in Plant Science, 13,"},{t:"h",x:"904516. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.904516"},{t:"p",x:"Gangaraj, R., Kundu, A., Prakash, G., Das, A., Nagaraja, A., & Kamil, D. (2024). Profiling of bioactive secondary metabolites from Aspergillus niger against a guava wilt pathogen, Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii. Archives of Microbiology, 206(12), 473. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00203-024-04199-7"},{t:"p",x:"López-Díaz, C., Rahjoo, V., Sulyok, M., Ghionna, V., Martín-Vicente, A., Capilla, J., Di Pietro, A., & López-Berges, M. S. (2018). Fusaric acid contributes to virulence of Fusarium oxysporum on plant and mammalian hosts. Molecular Plant Pathology, 19(2), 440–453. https://doi.org/10.1111/mpp.12536 Ma, L.-J., van der Does, H. C., Borkovich, K. A., Coleman, J. J., Daboussi, M.-J., Di Pietro, A., Dufresne, M., Freitag, M., Grabherr, M., Henrissat, B., Houterman, P. M., Kang, S., Shim, W.-B., Woloshuk, C., Xie, X., Xu, J.-R., Antoniw, J., Baker, S. E., Bluhm, B. H., … Rep, M. (2010). Comparative genomics reveals mobile pathogenicity chromosomes in Fusarium. Nature, 464(7287), 367–373. https://doi.org/10.1038/nature08850 Mishra, R. K., Pandey, B. K., Singh, V., Mathew, A. J., Pathak, N., & Zeeshan, M. (2013). Molecular detection and genotyping of Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii isolates from different agro-ecological regions of India. Journal of Microbiology, 51(4), 405–412. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12275-013-2638-3 Ploetz, R. C. (2015). Management of Fusarium wilt of banana: A review with special reference to tropical race 4. Crop Protection, 73, 7–15. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cropro.2015.01.007"},{t:"p",x:"Shravani, V., Kiran Kumar, K. C., Mesta, R. K., Basavarajappa, M. P., Meti, S., & Waseem, M. A. (2025). Unleashing the synergistic interaction between Meloidogyne enterolobii and Fusarium sp. in guava wilt complex disease. Scientific Reports, 15(1), 34131. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97767-w Souza, R. M., Oliveira, D. F., Gomes, V. M., Viana, A. J. S., Silva, G. H., & Machado, A. R. T. (2023). Meloidogyne enterolobii-induced changes in guava root exudates are associated with root rotting caused by Neocosmospora falciformis. Journal of Nematology, 55(1), 20230055. van Dam, P., de Sain, M., ter Horst, A., van der Gragt, M., Rep, M., & Master, E. R. (2015). The effector repertoire of Fusarium oxysporum determines the tomato xylem proteome composition following infection. Frontiers in Microbiology, 6, 967. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2015.00967 van Gundy, S. D., Kirkpatrick, J. D., & Golden, J. (1977). The nature and role of metabolic leakage from root-knot nematode galls and infection by Rhizoctonia solani. Journal of Nematology, 9(2), 113–121. Vishwakarma, P. K., Vasugi, C., Umamaheswari, R., Sriram, S., Nandeesha, P., Sankaran, M., & Shivashankara, K. S. (2023). Screening of Psidium species and their interspecific hybrid progenies for resistance to Fusarium oxysporum f. sp. psidii and southern root-knot nematode. South African Journal of Botany, 155, 249–260. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sajb.2023.02.015"},{t:"p",x:"Zuriegat, Q., Wang, Y., & Liu, Z. (2021). Current progress on pathogenicity-related transcription factors in Fusarium oxysporum. Molecular Plant Pathology, 22(7), 882–895. https://doi.org/10.1111/mpp.13054"}],s={n:10,title:e,authors:i,pages:a,affiliations:o,blocks:t};export{o as affiliations,i as authors,t as blocks,s as default,n,a as pages,e as title};
